00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:16,500
 So it is just Docker.

161
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:21,000
 We upload your Docker file to S3, and I process from Lander, and then it's going to build your micro VM.

162
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:31,900
 But what's different from running a container or running an EC2 instance is when the Lander service creates that micro VM,

163
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:35,620
 it's going to run whatever code you specify and then create a snapshot.

164
00:11:36,600 --> 00:11:44,280
 And so when it's going to then run again, when it's going to start and initialize, it's just going to initialize from the state of that snapshot.

165
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:51,360
 So all the stuff and the working and everything that you may have done in your initialization step, you can save that durably before that actually runs.

166
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:55,980
 And so you can run these micro VMs for up to eight hours, and they can stop and start.

167
00:11:55,980 --> 00:11:57,280
 You only pay for when they're running.

168
00:11:57,720 --> 00:11:59,320
 You can bring them down when you need to.

169
00:12:00,140 --> 00:12:02,700
 And it's just a simple service.

170
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:04,860
 So eight hours continuously running.

171
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:10,980
 It could be a whole micro VM can run for eight hours, or it can be available for eight hours, and you stop and start it whenever you like.

172
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:12,440
 And you get to control the life cycle.

173
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:17,040
 So you choose when to launch it, when to suspend it, when to resume it, and all that kind of thing.

174
00:12:17,260 --> 00:12:27,820
 So when you do suspend it within that eight hours, nothing gets lost, and then when you restart the micro VM automatically, or via a schedule or some kind of thing, it'll just pick up where it's left off.

175
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:37,480
 If you do need to run it for longer than eight hours, then you do need to then persist state to another kind of service, and people are using things like S3 files, or maybe it's just storing stuff in your database.

176
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:45,500
 So what it gives you, in terms of compute, you can select some different compute options, you know, from half a gig up to 32 gig of RAM.

177
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:55,580
 But the third one down is actually what is really cool, because you configure a base amount of memory and CPU, and then micro VMs allows you to automatically burst up to four times for that.

178
00:12:55,840 --> 00:13:02,300
 So you're going to pay for the base while it's running, and then that burst will just charge you just for that burst when that happens.

179
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:11,660
 So again, you don't have to then size your micro VM for your peak throughput, just what you need to guarantee it, and then that burst functionality is still built in with a micro VM.

